sections.s2_technical:
Spring Boot resolves properties in a strict priority order (highest wins): (1) command-line arguments (`--server.port=9090`), (2) JVM system properties (`-Dserver.port=9090`), (3) OS environment variables (`SERVER_PORT=9090` — note the UPPER_SNAKE_CASE mapping), (4) profile-specific application-{profile}.yml, (5) application.yml (base), (6) @PropertySource annotations, (7) default values in `@Value("${key:default}")`.

```java
// Reading properties — three approaches:
// 1. @Value — simple, inline
@Value("${bank.interest-rate:0.065}")
private BigDecimal interestRate;

// 2. @ConfigurationProperties — typed, validated, immutable (RECOMMENDED)
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "bank")
public record BankConfig(BigDecimal interestRate, int maxRetries, Duration timeout) {}
// Validates at startup: missing required field → app won't start. No runtime surprise.

// 3. Environment.getProperty — programmatic
@Autowired Environment env;
String port = env.getProperty("server.port", "8080");
```

Profiles activate configuration variants. `spring.profiles.active=prod` loads `application-prod.yml` which overrides base `application.yml`. Common patterns: `application-dev.yml` (H2 in-memory, DEBUG logging), `application-prod.yml` (PostgreSQL, WARN logging, connection pool tuning). Profiles can be activated via environment variable `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod` — no code change between environments.

```yaml
# application.yml (base — shared config)
bank:
  name: National Bank
  max-retries: 3

# application-dev.yml (dev overrides)
spring.datasource.url: jdbc:h2:mem:devdb

# application-prod.yml (prod overrides)
spring.datasource.url: jdbc:postgresql://prod-db:5432/bankdb
```

`@ConfigurationProperties` is preferred over `@Value` for production because: it is TYPE-SAFE (wrong type → startup failure), VALIDATED (add `@Validated` + `@NotNull`/`@Min` annotations), IMMUTABLE (use Java record), and TESTABLE (inject the config object in tests). `@Value` is fine for simple one-off values.

